---
title: "Aggregation - Configure Columns"
enterprise: true
---

Columns can be configured to aggregate data for each level of row grouping or tree data.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Enabling Aggregation" name="enable-aggregation" /%}

## Enabling Aggregation

An aggregation function can be applied to a column by setting the `aggFunc` grid option to one of:
`"sum"`, `"min"`, `"max"`, `"first"`, `"last"`, `"count"`, or `"avg"`.

The example above demonstrates the following configuration:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'sum' },
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'avg' },
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'count' },
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'min' },
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'max' },
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'first' },
        { field: 'total', aggFunc: 'last' },
        // ... other column definitions
    ],
};
```

{% note %}
The built-in functions will support `bigint` values if you have them in your data, but the `avg`
function will lose precision as it can only use integer arithmetic if `bigint` is used.
{% /note %}

## Configuring via the UI

Enable users to configure aggregation functions on a column using the [Columns Tool Panel](./tool-panel-columns/)
and [Column Menu](./column-menu/) by setting the `enableValue` column definition property to `true`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Directly Supplied Aggregation Functions" name="user-configured" /%}

The example above demonstrates the following configuration:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'bronze', enableValue: true },
        { field: 'silver', enableValue: true },
        { field: 'gold', enableValue: true },
        { field: 'total', enableValue: true },
        // ... other column definitions
    ],
    sideBar: 'columns',
};
```

### Reordering Values

Right-click a pill in the **Values** section of the Columns Tool Panel and choose **Move Up** or **Move Down** to reorder it without dragging. With a pill focused, {% kbd "⇧ Shift" /%}+{% kbd "F10" /%} or the context-menu key opens the same menu. Use `getColumnDropZoneMenuItems` with `source: 'aggregation'` to customise or suppress this menu. See [Drop Zone Menus](./tool-panel-columns-customising/#drop-zone-menus) for details.

### Allowed Functions

To restrict the aggregation functions that can be applied to a column, set the `allowedAggFuncs` column
definition property to an array of allowed aggregation function names. The functions appear in the order specified in the array.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Allowed Aggregations" name="user-configured-allowed-aggregations" /%}

The following configuration is an example demonstrating limiting a column to only allow the `"first"` and `"last"`
aggregation functions:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'total', enableValue: true, allowedAggFuncs: ['first', 'last'] },
        // ... other column definitions
    ],
};
```

### Default Function

When right clicked in the [Column Tool Panel](./tool-panel-columns) or dragged into the aggregation panel, the `"sum"` aggregation
function is applied to the column. This default can be changed by setting the `defaultAggFunc` column definition property.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Default Aggregation" name="user-configured-default-aggregations" /%}

The example above demonstrates the following configuration:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'total', enableValue: true, defaultAggFunc: 'avg' },
        // ... other column definitions
    ],
};
```

## Transforming Aggregated Values

To change the value an aggregated column shows on group rows, set the `aggValueGetter` column definition property
to a function. It is called for group rows, group total rows, the grand total row and tree data parent rows while
the column is aggregated, and returns the value of the cell. The aggregation function and its name in the header are
kept.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Aggregated Value Getter" name="agg-value-getter" /%}

The example above shows a weighted price per unit on group rows, derived from the `revenue` and `units` aggregates,
while the `unitPrice` column keeps its `avg` aggregation function:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'units', aggFunc: 'sum' },
        { field: 'revenue', aggFunc: 'sum' },
        {
            field: 'unitPrice',
            aggFunc: 'avg',
            aggValueGetter: (params) => params.aggData.revenue / params.aggData.units,
        },
        // ... other column definitions
    ],
};
```

The function receives the same parameters as a `valueGetter`, plus:

- `value`: the value the cell shows without `aggValueGetter`. Return it to keep that value.
- `aggFunc`: the column's active aggregation function.
- `aggData`: the row's computed aggregates, keyed by column ID. With the Server-Side Row Model this is
  `undefined`, and `value` is read from the row's data.
- `aggValue`: this column's computed aggregate, `undefined` with the Server-Side Row Model. For the built-in `avg`
  and `count` functions, `value` and `aggValue` are result objects: read their `value` property for the number.

The returned value is used everywhere the grid reads the cell value, including export, the clipboard,
`api.getCellValue`, and sorting and filtering in the Client-Side Row Model. With the Server-Side Row Model, the server
sorts and filters group rows. The returned value is not stored, so parent groups aggregate the computed aggregates of
their children, and the `avg` function still weights each child group by its row count.

The grid cannot reverse an `aggValueGetter`, so its built-in distribution cannot turn a value typed into a group
cell back into an aggregate. When the column is also [editable on group rows](./grouping-edit/), group cells are
editable only when `groupRowValueSetter` is a function. The function receives the typed value as `newValue` and the
value the cell showed as `oldValue`, both in the getter's units. It decides what to write to the descendant rows, for
example by reversing the getter on both and passing the results to `distributeGroupValue`. When that distribution
writes a child group, the function is called again with `distributing: true`, and `newValue` and `oldValue` are then
stored aggregates, so reverse the getter only when `distributing` is `false`. Here `reverse` is the app's function
that undoes its getter:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
groupRowValueSetter: (params) =>
    distributeGroupValue(
        params.distributing
            ? params
            : { ...params, newValue: reverse(params.newValue), oldValue: reverse(params.oldValue) }
    ),
```

The grid does not write the edited value into the group row's own data, as the function decides what to write.
With `groupRowEditable` or `groupRowValueSetter` set but no function `groupRowValueSetter`, group cells in the
column are not editable while it is aggregated, `setDataValue` on a group row leaves the data unchanged, and the grid
logs a warning. Leaf rows stay editable. With neither set, `setDataValue` writes the cell's field as it does for a
column with a `valueGetter`.

`aggValueGetter` is not called for leaf rows, group columns or pivot result columns. An open group whose aggregate is
shown on its total row displays no value, so the getter is not called to display it.

## Editing Aggregated Columns

Aggregated columns can be made editable at the group row level, allowing users to edit a group total and
distribute the change to descendant rows. Set `groupRowEditable` on the column and configure a
`groupRowValueSetter` to control how the edited value is distributed. The built-in distribution
automatically handles all standard aggregation functions.

See [Editing Group Rows](./grouping-edit/) for full details.

## Omit Function Name in Header

To omit the aggregation function name from the column header, set the `suppressAggFuncInHeader` grid option to `true`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Suppress Aggregation Function in Header" name="suppress-func-name" /%}

The example above demonstrates the following configuration:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    suppressAggFuncInHeader: true,
};
```
